        "priority": "routine",
        "code": {"coding": [{"system": "http://snomed.info/sct", "code": "306206005", "display": "Referral to general practitioner"}], "text": "Referral to general practitioner"},
        "subject": {"reference": "Patient/demo-patient-3"},
        "encounter": {"reference": "Encounter/demo-case3-enc"},
        "authoredOn": "2026-06-07T07:40:00Z",
        "reasonCode": [{"text": "Productive cough for three weeks, worsening"}],
        "note": [
          {"text": "HPI: 70-year-old man with COPD reporting three weeks of a productive cough with green sputum, now associated with increased breathlessness and rescue-inhaler use. Oxygen saturation 95% at last reading. Presentation suggests a likely lower respiratory tract infection or COPD exacerbation warranting prompt GP assessment for examination and possible antibiotics or steroids."},
          {"text": "Action: Book a GP appointment within 24-48 hours for chest examination."},
          {"text": "Action: Continue rescue inhaler; bring current medications to the appointment."},
          {"text": "Action: Attend urgent care or call emergency services if breathing becomes severe, lips turn blue, or confusion develops."},
          {"text": "Guideline: 0.84|COPD exacerbation triage (NICE NG115 summary)|An increase in cough, sputum volume or purulence, and breathlessness in a patient with COPD suggests an exacerbation; prompt clinical assessment is advised to consider antibiotics, oral corticosteroids, and escalation if hypoxia or systemic features are present."},
          {"text": "QR: demo-case3-qr"}
        ]
      }
    },

